Definition 8.3 says that a retraction \(a\colon E\to E\) is a projection when \(a\sqsubseteq 1_E\), and that it is finitary when its fixed-point set is isomorphic to a domain; the preceding remark is that every retraction coming from \(D\triangleleft E\) satisfies \(a\sqsubseteq 1_E\), while Example 8.4 builds a retraction from the two-point system \(O=\{\{0\},\{0,1\}\}\) that is not a projection in general, then builds the strict and smash projections. `IsProjection` is the conjunction of being a retraction and lying under `idMap`, `IsFinitary` is an order isomorphism from the fixed-point subtype to the element poset of some neighbourhood system, and `FinitaryProjection` requires both. `retractionOfSubsystem_le_idMap` is the remark: a related pair supplies an intermediate subsystem neighbourhood \(Y\) with \(X\subseteq Y\subseteq Z\), so \(X\subseteq Z\). `isFinitary_retractionOfSubsystem` takes the subsystem itself as the witnessing domain, and `isProjection_retractionOfSubsystem` with `isFinitaryProjection_retractionOfSubsystem` pack the two clauses. In Example 8.4(a), `O` has neighbourhoods the singleton of zero and the full two-point set, with the full set as master. `check` relates \(X\) to \(Y\) when \(Y\) is the full set or \(X\) differs from the master, and `mem_toElementMap_check` says the value is bottom exactly on bottom and top on every other element. `fade₂` relates an \(O\)-neighbourhood and a \(D\)-neighbourhood to an output that is either the master or the passage of the \(D\)-neighbourhood through the input \(\{0\}\), so `mem_toElementMap₂_fade` gives bottom when the \(O\)-element is bottom and the second input otherwise. `a` is fade after the pairing of check with the constant map at an element \(u\), `a_bot` and `a_of_ne_bot` give \(a(\bot)=\bot\) and \(a(x)=u\) for \(x\neq\bot\), `isRetraction_a` is the retraction law when \(u\neq\bot\), and `fixIso` identifies the fixed-point set with \(O\) by sending an element of \(O\) to `fixOfO` and a fixed point back through check. The further statement that this retraction fails to be a projection in general has no theorem. In Example 8.4(b), `strictRetraction` sends \(f\) to the function taking \(y\) to fade of check of \(y\) applied to \(f(y)\), and `example84b_strict` shows that it is a projection whose fixed points are order-isomorphic to the strict function space. `smashRetraction` is fade of check of the first coordinate applied to fade of check of the second coordinate applied to the pair, and `example84b_smash` shows that it is a projection on the product whose fixed points are order-isomorphic to the smash product. **Theorem:** partial — the two clauses, the subsystem retraction, and the check, fade, strict, and smash constructions match the note, and the claim that \(a\) is not a projection in general is absent. **Proof:** partial — the intermediate-neighbourhood inclusion, the case analyses of check and fade, the retraction and fixed-point isomorphisms, and the projection packaging of strict and smash are the arguments above; the negative projection claim has no argument.
